<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="3ca6913">&lt;p&gt;
    Perhaps the most difficult part of issue management is deciding when to document something as an issue.&amp;nbsp; Many
    questions and problems are raised during the course of a project, some of these may be simply dealt with,&amp;nbsp; others
    may require more formal documentation, evaluation, and approval.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Deciding whether to document an issue is primarily a judgment call. However, if you can answer &quot;yes&quot; to two or more of
    the following questions, the matter should probably be documented as an issue:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Will it have a significant impact on the project (i.e., cause a delay, change the direction, impair the solution,
        hinder the quality, alter the model or deliverable content, or increase the cost)?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is it relevant to the project? Often there are issues that have nothing to do with the outcome of your project.
        They should be documented in another issue log or the program issue log, but not in the project issue log.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Will it require a decision by the project manager or project sponsor?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Should the decision be made visible? When a decision must be made on a controversial question, you may want to
        document the question as an issue and the decision as a resolution. Visibility can aid in the decision-making
        process, and act as an audit trail if the decision is later questioned.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Does it need further clarification or investigation?
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
